From 2ab2f9d7eed513f9bb2edb1431bef037e5bd8a56 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matt Brocklehurst <matt@mattbrocklehurst.co.uk>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 17:31:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ARM: dts: bcm2836: add peripheral alias to dma-ranges for
 MMIO DMA

The soc node has a single dma-ranges entry covering only the RAM
window (0x0-0x3EFFFFFF -> VC bus 0xC0000000-0xFEFFFFFF). Peripherals
under this node inherit dev->dma_range_map from that entry alone.

With the DMA_ATTR_MMIO unification in the 6.18 dma-mapping refactor
(dma_map_phys/dma_direct_map_phys), drivers that DMA-map their own
MMIO register block (e.g. bcm2835-dma slave transfers to sdhosts
FIFO register) now route that physical address through the same
dev->dma_range_map translation as RAM buffers. sdhosts FIFO sits at
0x3F202040, physical outside the RAM-only window, so the lookup in
translate_phys_to_dma() finds no matching range and returns the
no-translation sentinel (dma_addr_t)-1. dma_capable() correctly
rejects that, and the driver never completes a transfer, so
disk_scan_partitions() finds zero partitions on the root device and
the kernel panics unable to mount root.

Confirmed via gdb against QEMU (raspi2b machine, kernel built with
CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_DWARF5): dma_direct_map_phys() is reached with
phys=0x3f202040, attrs=DMA_ATTR_MMIO, and dma_capable() returns false
because dma_addr == DMA_MAPPING_ERROR (0xffffffff), not because of an
arithmetic overflow against bus_dma_limit as the WARN message alone
suggests.

Fix by adding a second dma-ranges entry for the peripheral block,
using the same 0x3f000000 -> 0x7e000000 alias the sibling ranges
property (immediately above) already declares for CPU-side MMIO
access to the same peripherals -- this DMA-side entry was simply
missing, not a new value invented for this fix.

Verified end to end: kernel boots, both partitions are detected, and
the ext4 root filesystem mounts cleanly under QEMU raspi2b, where it
previously panicked at VFS: Unable to mount root fs.

Same dma-ranges line (byte-identical) exists in bcm2837.dtsi, so this
likely affects Pi 3-class boards too -- see
https://github.com/raspberrypi/linux/issues/7136, where the reported
fix (daa7a3588ce0) restored phys_to_dma() translation for MMIO
addresses but, per this analysis, does not by itself cover physical
addresses outside the RAM-only dma-ranges window.

Signed-off-by: Matt Brocklehurst <matt@mattbrocklehurst.co.uk>
---
 arch/arm/boot/dts/broadcom/bcm2836.dtsi | 3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm/boot/dts/broadcom/bcm2836.dtsi b/arch/arm/boot/dts/broadcom/bcm2836.dtsi
index 783fe624ba68..4ab7769c056a 100644
--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/broadcom/bcm2836.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/broadcom/bcm2836.dtsi
@@ -8,7 +8,8 @@ / {
 	soc {
 		ranges = <0x7e000000 0x3f000000 0x1000000>,
 			 <0x40000000 0x40000000 0x00001000>;
-		dma-ranges = <0xc0000000 0x00000000 0x3f000000>;
+		dma-ranges = <0xc0000000 0x00000000 0x3f000000>,
+			     <0x7e000000 0x3f000000 0x01000000>;
 
 		local_intc: interrupt-controller@40000000 {
 			compatible = "brcm,bcm2836-l1-intc";
-- 
2.43.0

